From: Bharath SM <bharathsm@microsoft.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Mar 2026 16:00:49 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] smb: client: fix in-place encryption corruption in
 SMB2_write()

commit d78840a6a38d312dc1a51a65317bb67e46f0b929 upstream.

[ Backport: 6.1 has no rq_iter, and the transform shares rq_iov. Copy the payload to a kvmalloc bounce buffer when encryption is required. ]
[ Backport: In 6.1 the caller's buffer can be page cache data, so the copy also keeps the page cache plaintext. ]

SMB2_write() places write payload in iov[1..n] as part of rq_iov.
smb3_init_transform_rq() pointer-shares rq_iov, so crypt_message()
encrypts iov[1] in-place, replacing the original plaintext with
ciphertext. On a replayable error, the retry sends the same iov[1]
which now contains ciphertext instead of the original data,
resulting in corruption.

The corruption is most likely to be observed when connections are
unstable, as reconnects trigger write retries that re-send the
already-encrypted data.

This affects SFU mknod, MF symlinks, etc. On kernels before
6.10 (prior to the netfs conversion), sync writes also used
this path and were similarly affected. The async write path
wasn't unaffected as it uses rq_iter which gets deep-copied.

Fix by moving the write payload into rq_iter via iov_iter_kvec(),
so smb3_init_transform_rq() deep-copies it before encryption.

Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org #6.3+
Acked-by: Henrique Carvalho <henrique.carvalho@suse.com>
Acked-by: Shyam Prasad N <sprasad@microsoft.com>
Acked-by: Paulo Alcantara (Red Hat) <pc@manguebit.org>
Signed-off-by: Bharath SM <bharathsm@microsoft.com>
Signed-off-by: Steve French <stfrench@microsoft.com>
Signed-off-by: Igor Pecovnik <igor@armbian.com>
---
 fs/smb/client/smb2pdu.c | 32 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+)

diff --git a/fs/smb/client/smb2pdu.c b/fs/smb/client/smb2pdu.c
index e69b4bb9dc91..63aeb574240f 100644
--- a/fs/smb/client/smb2pdu.c
+++ b/fs/smb/client/smb2pdu.c
@@ -4897,6 +4897,10 @@ SMB2_write(const unsigned int xid, struct cifs_io_parms *io_parms,
 	int flags = 0;
 	unsigned int total_len;
 	struct TCP_Server_Info *server;
+	struct kvec enc_iov[2];
+	void *enc_buf = NULL;
+	size_t enc_len = 0;
+	int i;
 
 	*nbytes = 0;
 
@@ -4942,10 +4946,38 @@ SMB2_write(const unsigned int xid, struct cifs_io_parms *io_parms,
 	rqst.rq_iov = iov;
 	rqst.rq_nvec = n_vec + 1;
 
+	/*
+	 * crypt_message() encrypts rq_iov in place. Send a copy of the
+	 * payload, so that the caller's data stays plaintext for retries.
+	 */
+	if (flags & CIFS_TRANSFORM_REQ) {
+		for (i = 1; i <= n_vec; i++)
+			enc_len += iov[i].iov_len;
+	}
+	if (enc_len) {
+		enc_buf = kvmalloc(enc_len, GFP_KERNEL);
+		if (!enc_buf) {
+			cifs_small_buf_release(req);
+			return -ENOMEM;
+		}
+		enc_len = 0;
+		for (i = 1; i <= n_vec; i++) {
+			memcpy(enc_buf + enc_len, iov[i].iov_base,
+			       iov[i].iov_len);
+			enc_len += iov[i].iov_len;
+		}
+		enc_iov[0] = iov[0];
+		enc_iov[1].iov_base = enc_buf;
+		enc_iov[1].iov_len = enc_len;
+		rqst.rq_iov = enc_iov;
+		rqst.rq_nvec = 2;
+	}
+
 	rc = cifs_send_recv(xid, io_parms->tcon->ses, server,
 			    &rqst,
 			    &resp_buftype, flags, &rsp_iov);
 	rsp = (struct smb2_write_rsp *)rsp_iov.iov_base;
+	kvfree(enc_buf);
 
 	if (rc) {
 		trace_smb3_write_err(xid,
